Thanks — I can walk through this and give estimates, explain options, and list the forms you’ll likely need. I’m not your tax preparer, so I recommend you confirm these numbers with a CPA or tax software before filing.

Summary of your facts (what I used)
- Filing status: Head of Household (2023)
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment/consulting income: $15,000 (4 clients)
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover savings) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year (no mortgage)
- Home size: 140 sq ft exclusive office of 690 sq ft total → business % = 140/690 ≈ 20.29%
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (FedLoan, 2016–2020)
- Charitable cash gift: $2,000 (Local United Way)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350 (need clarification whether these are business expenses for consulting or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job)

Immediate conclusions (high level)
- Standard deduction (Head of Household, 2023) = $20,800. With the facts shown, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction rather than itemize (your only confirmed itemizable amount is $2,000 charity).
- Student loan interest deduction likely not available because your MAGI is above the phaseout (see details below).
- Because you have self‑employment income, you should file Schedule C (profit/loss), pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can claim a home office deduction for the consulting business (either simplified or actual).
- Some self‑employment benefits apply (half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction). You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995) — likely available given your approximate income.

Detailed calculations & treatment

1) Home office deduction (two methods)
- Business % = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.

Simplified method:
- 140 sq ft × $5/sq ft = $700 deduction.

Actual-expense method:
- Rent portion attributable to business = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115.
- If you have utilities, renters insurance, etc., their business share could increase this deduction.
- For self‑employed taxpayers using actual expenses, you generally file Form 8829 to calculate the deduction. With the rent alone, the actual method (≈$5,115) is much larger than the simplified $700.

Important limits: The home-office deduction can’t reduce the net profit from the business below zero; excess home-office deduction may be carried forward. In your numbers, the actual-method deduction is allowable and would reduce your net self‑employment profit.

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments (approximate)
Assume the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the consulting activity (if they are employee unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible under current law — see question below).

If treated as business expenses, example calculation using actual home office:

- Net business profit = 15,000 − 1,350 (business expenses) − 5,115 (home office) ≈ $8,535.
- Self‑employment net earnings = 92.35% × $8,535 ≈ $7,884.
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $7,884 ≈ $1,206.
- Half of SE tax (≈ $603) is an adjustment to income (above the line) on Schedule 1. The other half is payable with your tax.

(If you use the simplified home office ($700) instead, net profit and SE tax would be higher — I can rerun that if you prefer.)

3) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500, but it phases out for single/head‑of‑household taxpayers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023. Your MAGI (with the numbers above) is above $90,000, so the deduction is fully phased out and you cannot claim it.

4) Charitable gift ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize. Since the standard deduction for HOH is $20,800 and you have only $2,000 of confirmed itemized deduction, you’ll take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

5) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your consulting. Rough estimate:
  - QBI ≈ your net Schedule C profit ≈ $8,535 → 20% × $8,535 ≈ $1,707 deduction
  - This deduction is taken after AGI/standard deduction (reported via Form 8995). Because your taxable income will be well below the QBI phase‑out thresholds, you should be able to claim it. This further lowers your taxable income.

6) Do you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Almost certainly take the standard deduction ($20,800) unless you have additional large deductible items (significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, or more charitable/other deductible amounts). Based on the items you listed, standard deduction is better.

7) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for half of SE tax, other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income, business expenses, and home office deduction (simplified method enters directly on Schedule C; actual method requires Form 8829)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — to claim the QBI deduction (most taxpayers use Form 8995)
- Keep Forms 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC you receive from clients, and 1099-INT for your interest income; your W-2 for wages.

State return
- Illinois IL‑1040 to report the same income to the state (and pay any state income tax owed).

Practical next steps / checklist
- Confirm whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are for your consulting (self‑employment) — if yes, deduct on Schedule C; if they are employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible.
- Decide whether to use the simplified ($700) or actual (~$5,115 from rent alone) method for the home office. If actual, gather rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, and other shared household bills for 2023; keep copies/receipts and use Form 8829.
- Gather 1099s, W‑2, bank/broker interest statements, receipts for the charity, and records of business expenses.
- Use tax software or a CPA to compute exact AGI, SE tax, QBI deduction, and income tax. I can walk through more precise estimates or produce a worksheet if you want.

Would you like me to:
- Recalculate using the simplified home-office method for comparison?
- Re-run the numbers if the $1,350 expenses are NOT business-related?
- Draft a short checklist/email you can send to your tax preparer listing documents and questions?

