Quick numbers
- Total gross income (2023): W-2 $82,350 + consulting $15,000 + interest $1,780 + interest $195 = $99,325.
- Student loan interest you paid: $2,700 (note: the deductible maximum for 2023 is $2,500 — see caveat below).
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year.
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive / 690 sq ft total = 140/690 ≈ 20.3% of home.

How to treat each item
- W-2 wages and interest: Report as usual on Form 1040. Taxable interest from Discover + Fidelity is reportable; because your total interest ($1,780 + $195 = $1,975) exceeds $1,500 you will likely need Schedule B.
- Consulting income ($15,000): Report on Schedule C (self-employment income). Business-related expenses (see below) reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Unreimbursed expenses $1,350: If these are business expenses for your consulting work (equipment, supplies, travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses (for an employer), they are NOT deductible for 2023 due to TCJA.
- Home office: Because you have self-employment income, you can claim a home-office deduction for the portion used exclusively and regularly for business.
  - Actual-expense method example (using rent only as a simple illustration): 20.3% × $25,200 rent ≈ $5,112 deductible rent portion. You can also allocate utilities and other eligible expenses similarly.
  - Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (cap applies). You may prefer the actual-expense method if it produces a larger deduction. If using actual expenses you’d typically file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C; if using the simplified method, report it directly on Schedule C without Form 8829.
- Student loan interest $2,700: The deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (you don’t have to itemize). The maximum allowed in 2023 is $2,500, and the deduction phases out based on MAGI (so your allowed amount could be limited). With total income ~ $99k, you may be in or above the phaseout range — check MAGI rules or ask your preparer to confirm. The deduction is reported on Form 1040 / Schedule 1 (adjustments).
- Charitable donation $2,000: Only deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you can’t also deduct this on Schedule A. For 2023 the standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction (Head of Household, 2023): $20,800.
- Likely itemized deductions you reported: $2,000 charitable + any state/local taxes and mortgage interest or property taxes (you didn’t list these). Without substantial mortgage interest, property tax, or large state tax payments, your itemized total will likely be well below $20,800.
- Tentative conclusion: Take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (significant mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other deductible expenses) that push itemized deductions above $20,800.

Self-employment tax and related items
- You’ll owe self-employment tax on net Schedule C profit; calculate using Schedule SE.
- Half of your self-employment tax is an above-the-line deduction (Schedule 1).
- Consider estimated tax payments if you didn’t have sufficient withholding to cover income + SE tax.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest, half SE tax if applicable)
  - Schedule B (interest/dividends) — because interest > $1,500
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your consulting income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (if using actual home-office expense method) OR use the simplified method on Schedule C
  - Schedule A only if you itemize (likely unnecessary given the standard deduction amount)
- State (Illinois):
  - IL-1040 (state return) — Illinois has a separate filing requirement and flat rate; include your state-taxable income, etc.
- Other: 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for consulting clients if you received them; 1099-INT from the banks (you should receive these).

Action items / questions I need to finalize guidance
- Do you have mortgage interest, property tax payments, or significant state income tax withheld or paid? (That could change standard vs. itemize decision.)
- Were the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your consulting work? If yes, confirm types (supplies, travel, phone, software, etc.).
- Were any of the consulting clients employees of someone else (i.e., did you receive 1099s)? Any business-related receipts/bank statements?
- Would you like me to forward this summary (and your details) to vince2000@gmail.com or draft/send an email to a tax preparer? I’ll only send if you say go.

