Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical summary for your 2023 filing with estimates and next steps. I’ll also ask a few quick clarifying questions at the end so I can fine-tune the advice.

Quick facts you gave
- Filing status: Head of household (Chicago, IL)
- W-2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employed consulting income: $15,000 (4 clients)
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover) + $195 (Fidelity)
- Rent: $2,100/month = $25,200/year (total home 690 sq ft)
- Home office: 140 sq ft, used exclusively for business
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (FedLoan)
- Charitable cash gift: $2,000 (United Way) — you have receipt
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350

What you can and cannot deduct (summary)
- W-2 and interest income: Fully reportable on Form 1040. You should receive W-2 and 1099-INTs for the bank/fidelity interest.
- Self-employment income: Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against that $15,000.
- Home office: Deductible only for self-employed business use (not for an employee unless you are a statutory exception). You have two methods:
  - Regular method (recommended here based on the numbers): allocate shared home expenses by the business-use percentage (140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%). Rent deduction ≈ $25,200 * 20.3% ≈ $5,116 plus a proportionate share of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc. If you don’t provide utilities/other amounts I used only rent as an example.
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 * $5 = $700.
  - Which to pick? Regular method will likely give a larger deduction in your case (rent-only example: ≈$5,116 vs $700). Use regular if you can document shared expenses (rent, utilities).
  - If you use the regular method you’ll normally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach results to Schedule C. Simplified method does not require Form 8829.
- Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses ($1,350): If these are expenses related to your self‑employment (consulting) they can be deducted on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses as an employee (W-2 job), those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Self-employment tax: Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest ($2,700): The maximum adjustment for student loan interest is $2,500. However, it phases out based on MAGI for 2023: for single/head of household the phaseout range is $75,000–$90,000. Your rough MAGI (before adjustments) is around $99k (82,350 + 15,000 + interest 1,975 = ~99,325), so you are likely above the phaseout and may not be eligible for any student loan interest deduction unless other above-the-line adjustments significantly reduce MAGI. The self-employment deductions (business expenses and half of SE tax) could lower MAGI — we can check exact numbers if you want.
- Charitable contribution ($2,000): Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). For 2023 the standard deduction for head of household is $20,800. Unless you have additional itemizable things (state & local taxes up to $10k, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.) your $2,000 contribution alone is unlikely to push you above the standard deduction.
- State income tax / SALT: If you paid Illinois state income tax or property tax, that can go on Schedule A but is capped at $10,000 (SALT cap). You didn’t list those amounts — they matter for the itemize vs standard decision.

Estimated example (to show impact)
(Assumptions: you choose regular home office and the $1,350 are business expenses)
- Business income: $15,000
- Home office rent portion (rent only): ≈ $5,116
- Other business expenses: $1,350
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $15,000 − $5,116 − $1,350 = $8,534
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 8,534) ≈ $1,205 → half deductible (~$603)
- That reduces AGI somewhat, but rough AGI still likely ≈ $98–99k, so student loan interest deduction likely phased out.
If instead you used the simplified home office ($700) or if the $1,350 are employee unreimbursed (not business expenses), your net business income (and self-employment tax) will be different. I can run variants if you want.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — used for some adjustments like student loan interest if allowed
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the half-SE-tax deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular home-office method
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (probably not in your case unless you have additional itemizable items)
- Supporting documents to have on hand: W-2, 1099‑INTs, 1099‑NEC (if any), Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement), receipts for charitable donation, receipts for business expenses, proof of home-office exclusive use and square footage (photos/diagram), rent statements, utility bills.

Recommendations / next steps
1. Decide whether those $1,350 are business expenses. If yes: put them on Schedule C. If no (employee unreimbursed), they’re nondeductible.
2. Use the regular home-office method (Form 8829) — it looks to give a larger deduction here given your rent — provided you can document shared expenses (rent, utilities). If you prefer simplicity or have trouble documenting, use the simplified method ($700).
3. Likely take the standard deduction ($20,800 for HOH) unless you have other large itemizable deductions (SALT, mortgage interest, large medical).
4. Gather documents: W-2, 1099s, 1098‑E, receipts for donations and business expenses, rent statements and proof of home office area.
5. If you want, I can run a precise calculation with the exact utility amounts, any state/local tax paid, or any other business expenses to confirm student loan interest eligibility and whether itemizing helps.

Questions so I can finalize precise numbers
- Are the $1,350 expenses related to your consulting work (self-employment)? If yes, what are they (travel, supplies, software, etc.)?
- Any other business expenses or deductions not listed (internet portion, cell phone, supplies, equipment, home utilities amounts)?
- Did you pay any Illinois state income tax or property tax in 2023 (amount)?
- Do you prefer I compute a full estimated tax return summary (AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax + self-employment tax)?

Privacy / forwarding
- I can draft or send email summaries or forward this (or your tax questions) to someone (e.g., a preparer). Do you want me to forward this information to a tax supervisor or preparer? If yes, please confirm the recipient and that you authorize sharing. I will not forward anything without your permission.

If you want, I can:
- Run exact numbers if you give any missing figures (utilities, state taxes paid, confirmation of the $1,350), or
