- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction (Head of Household = $20,800 for 2023). Your itemizable deductions (you listed $2,000 charitable) are far below the standard deduction unless you have significant deductible state taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts.
- Your $15,000 consulting is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. That allows business expense deductions (including the home‑office and the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses) which will reduce your net self‑employment profit.
- Claim the home‑office deduction on Schedule C. Using the actual-expense method looks substantially better than the simplified method for your numbers, but the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your business net profit.
- The student‑loan interest deduction will likely be disallowed because your MAGI (modified AGI) exceeds the phaseout ceiling for Head of Household (phaseout ends at $90,000 for 2023). Based on the numbers below, you’re above that limit.
- You’ll also owe self‑employment tax on the net profit; you can deduct one‑half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

Detailed calculations (estimates using numbers you provided)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment gross (consulting): $15,000
- Interest income (Discover + Fidelity): $1,780 + $195 = $1,975
- Total gross income = 82,350 + 15,000 + 1,975 = $99,325

Home office calculation (actual-expense method)
- Home size: 690 sq ft; office = 140 sq ft → business-use % = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%
- Rent: $2,100/mo → $25,200/yr
- Home‑office portion of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,116
- Business expenses you listed (unreimbursed): $1,350
- Total business expenses ≈ $5,116 + $1,350 = $6,466
- Estimated Schedule C net profit = 15,000 − 6,466 = $8,534 (approx.)

Self‑employment tax and adjustment
- SE tax ≈ net profit × 92.35% × 15.3% ≈ 8,534 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $1,206
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $603 (an above‑the‑line adjustment)

Estimated AGI
- AGI ≈ wages + interest + Schedule C net profit − (half SE deduction)
- ≈ 82,350 + 1,975 + 8,534 − 603 = ≈ $92,256

Student loan interest deduction
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 but is phased out for Head of Household when MAGI is between $75,000 and $90,000 for 2023. With estimated AGI ≈ $92,256, you are above the phaseout, so you likely cannot take the student‑loan interest deduction.

Standard vs. itemize
- Standard deduction (HOH 2023) = $20,800
- Your known itemizable amount is $2,000 charitable + unknown state/local taxes and mortgage interest. Unless you have around $20k of other itemizable deductions (state income/property tax up to SALT cap of $10k, mortgage interest, etc.) you should take the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll need
- Form 1040 (individual return)
