Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I’m not a CPA, but I can walk through how these items are generally treated for federal 2023 taxes and give estimates so you can decide next steps (or share with a tax preparer). I’ll highlight what I need to be more precise.

Summary of your reported numbers
- W-2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment/consulting income: $15,000 (4 clients)
- Interest: Discover $1,780 + Fidelity $195 = $1,975
- Total gross income ≈ $99,325

Key issues and conclusions (high level)
1. Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your only listed itemized deduction is $2,000 charity (Local United Way). Unless you have substantial state & local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or more charitable gifts, your itemized total will almost certainly be less than $20,800.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction.

2. Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction has a $2,500 annual cap and phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with Modified AGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (2023).
- With your reported income and the effect of self‑employment, your MAGI is likely above $90,000, which generally disqualifies the deduction. So you probably cannot deduct the student loan interest for 2023.
- Note: Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible — you need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for the amount paid in 2023.

3. Self‑employment income and taxes
- You must report the $15,000 on Schedule C (profit or loss from business).
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net profit (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% of net earnings (technically 92.35% of net profit × 15.3%). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).

4. Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may qualify for the home office deduction (must be regular and exclusive business use).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829 needed). Simpler, lower deduction.
  b) Actual expense method: prorate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business:
     - Business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
     - Rent allocable to business = $25,200/year × 20.29% ≈ $5,114 (plus apportioned utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.).
     - Using actual method you would generally file Form 8829 to compute the deduction.
- If your consulting expenses (including home office share and the $1,350 business expenses you listed) greatly reduce your net self‑employment profit, they could reduce SE tax and AGI. But even using the actual method, your AGI still looks to be near/above the student‑loan phaseout threshold (see estimates below).

Estimated numeric examples (rounded)
- Scenario A — Actual home office allocation (rent share ≈ $5,114) + $1,350 business expenses:
  - Net self‑employment profit ≈ $15,000 - $5,114 - $1,350 = $8,536
  - SE tax ≈ $1,206 → half SE deduction ≈ $603
  - AGI before student loan interest ≈ 82,350 + 8,536 + 1,975 - 603 = ≈ $92,258
  - This is above the $90k phaseout threshold for student loan interest, so the student loan deduction would likely be disallowed.

- Scenario B — Simplified home office ($700) + $1,350 business expenses:
  - Net self‑employment profit ≈ $15,000 - $700 - $1,350 = $12,950
  - SE tax ≈ $1,828 → half SE deduction ≈ $914
  - AGI before student loan interest ≈ 82,350 + 12,950 + 1,975 - 914 = ≈ $96,361
  - Also > $90k, so student loan interest would be disallowed.

Bottom line: with the income you gave, the student loan interest deduction is probably not available. The home office deduction is allowed for your self‑employment income and can be taken either via simplified or actual method (actual gives a larger rent‑based deduction in your case). You will likely take the standard deduction rather than itemizing.

Forms you’ll most likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for reporting some adjustments (student loan interest would go here if allowed)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (likely you won’t)
- Keep: Forms 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received them), W‑2, 1099‑INT (banks), 1098‑E (student loan interest, if any), receipts for charitable gifts, business receipts, home expense records.

Other practical points
- If you did not receive 1099s for your consulting income, you still must report the income.
- Keep documentation: receipts for business expenses, proof of exclusive home‑office use (floor plan/photos if needed), rent & utility bills, charitable donation receipts, and Form 1098‑E if you claim student loan interest.
- You may owe additional tax because of self‑employment tax. If you expect Owe > $1,000 when you file, consider making quarterly estimated payments for the current year.
- State tax: You’ll also file Illinois (IL-1040). IL has a flat rate and will start with federal AGI; there may be differences—check state rules or ask a tax pro.

Questions I need to be more precise
1. Are the “unreimbursed expenses” $1,350 business expenses for the consulting work (i.e., Schedule C), or are they employee unreimbursed expenses? (If the latter, they are not deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025.)
2. Did you pay $2,700 of student loan interest in 2023 (and do you have a 1098‑E for 2023), or is that an aggregate over multiple years?
3. Do you rent (I assumed yes because you listed monthly rent), or do you own the home (mortgage interest would change the analysis)?
4. Do you have significant state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, or other charitable gifts beyond the $2,000 listed?

If you want, I can:
- Draft a one‑page summary you can send to your tax preparer (or to vince2000@gmail.com) with these numbers and the documents you need to gather.
- Prepare a draft email requesting missing documents (1099s, 1098‑E, receipts) to Vince or your preparer.

Would you like me to draft that email or a summary for your tax preparer? If yes, tell me which recipient and any specific message to include.

