Summary answer (short)
- File Form 1040. Report consulting on Schedule C and self-employment tax on Schedule SE. Report interest income on Schedule B (total interest $1,975 > $1,500). Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment if you qualify, but based on the numbers you’ve given you’ll likely be above the MAGI phaseout for the full deduction. Most likely you should take the 2023 head-of-household standard deduction ($20,800) unless you have large additional itemizable deductions. The home-office deduction for your consulting is allowed if the 140 sq ft area is used exclusively and regularly for the business — you can use either the simplified method ($5 × 140 = $700) or the regular method (your business percentage × allowable home expenses); with your rent the regular method will probably give a larger deduction.

Detailed breakdown and actions

1) Income you reported (how it’s reported)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 — reported directly on Form 1040.
- Consulting income: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct business expenses related to that consulting on Schedule C (including home-office portion and legitimate business expenses).
- Interest income: $1,780 (Discover) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975 — report on Form 1040. Because total taxable interest > $1,500, attach Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).
- Note: You’ll also owe self-employment tax on net self‑employment income (see below) and can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

2) Home‑office deduction (self‑employment context)
You said: exclusive space = 140 sq ft; total home = 690 sq ft; rent = $2,100/month ($25,200/year).

- Business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Regular method (allocating a percentage of actual home expenses): if you only count rent, home‑office deduction = 20.29% × $25,200 ≈ $5,112 (plus a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you paid those and can allocate them).
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829 required).
- Which to choose? Regular method generally gives more here (≈$5,112 vs $700). For self-employed taxpayers the home-office deduction is reported on Schedule C; Form 8829 is used if you use the regular method to compute business-use-of-home expenses. If you’re a W‑2 employee and that space is for employment (not self-employment), the home-office deduction is not available for 2018–2025 — but because you have consulting income (self-employed), you can take it on the Schedule C for that business if the space is used exclusively for that consulting work.

3) Business / unreimbursed expenses
- The $1,350 you listed: if these are legitimate business expenses for your consulting (supplies, mileage, marketing, software, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a job where you’re a W‑2 employee, those are not deductible for 2018–2025.
- Combine those business expenses with your home-office deduction to compute net profit (or loss) on Schedule C. Net profit is subject to income tax and self-employment tax.

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- If you have net self-employment income, run Schedule SE to compute self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You can deduct half of your self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 of Form 1040).
- That half-SE tax deduction will reduce your AGI (and can affect phaseouts like student loan interest).

5) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- Student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (above-the-line) up to $2,500 for 2023, subject to MAGI phaseouts. For single/head-of-household filers the phaseout for 2023 begins at $75,000 and is complete at $90,000 of MAGI.
