- Other items: $2,700 student‑loan interest (you wrote FedLoan 2016–2020 — please confirm whether interest was paid in 2023 and whether you have Form 1098‑E for 2023), $2,000 charitable contribution (receipt), $1,350 expenses (please confirm whether these are business/unreimbursed employee expenses and whether they relate to consulting).

What you can and cannot deduct (rules + how they apply here)
- Student loan interest (Form 1040 adjustment up to $2,500): only deductible for interest actually paid in the tax year (reported on 1098‑E) and only if your MAGI is below the phaseout. For 2023 the phaseout for single / head of household is MAGI $75,000–$90,000. With the figures you gave your MAGI is likely above $90k, so you probably cannot take this deduction unless you reduce MAGI (see actions below). Also confirm whether the $2,700 was paid in 2023 — if not, it’s not deductible on the 2023 return.
- Charitable contribution ($2,000): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since Head of Household standard deduction for 2023 is $20,800, a $2,000 charitable gift alone will not beat the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: NOT deductible for employees for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). If the $1,350 are business expenses of your self-employed consulting work, then they ARE deductible on Schedule C (not Schedule A). Please confirm whether these are self-employment expenses.
- Self-employment business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. That includes direct business costs, supplies, software, travel for clients, etc.
- Home-office: deductible only if used regularly and exclusively for business and if the space is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). For a self-employed consultant you can take a home-office deduction. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Actual-expense method: allocate actual rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. by business-use percent (≈20.3%). With rent $25,200, rent portion for the office ≈ $25,200 × 20.3% ≈ $5,113. You’d also allocate a portion of utilities and other shared expenses; total actual deduction will likely be larger than the $700 simplified method in your situation. Use Form 8829 (reported via Schedule C) for the actual method. The home-office deduction reduces Schedule C net income, which in turn reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Self-employment tax (Schedule SE): you will owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self‑employment income (after business expenses). The SE tax is computed on 92.35% of net profit × 15.3%. You then get an “above‑the‑line” adjustment equal to half of the SE tax.
