Summary up front
- You’ll file Form 1040. Your W-2 and interest income go on the 1040; your consulting income (if you are self-employed/independent contractor) is reported on Schedule C with self‑employment tax on Schedule SE.
- Likely outcome: the Head‑of‑Household standard deduction for 2023 ($20,800) will be larger than the likely itemized deductions you listed (so standard deduction is probably better). Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction but may be phased out based on your MAGI — with the income numbers you provided you may be over the phaseout and thus not eligible. The home‑office deduction is available only if that consulting work is self‑employment (not as an employee) and can be taken either with the simplified method or the actual/allocated method (Form 8829 for actual).
- You may also be eligible for a Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of QBI) on your net self‑employment profit — this reduces taxable income and can be important, but rules can be a little complex.

1) Which items you can deduct (and where)
- W-2 wages: no deduction — taxed as wages on Form 1040.
- Consulting income ($15,000): report on Schedule C if you are self‑employed/independent contractor. On Schedule C you deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses can be deducted here only if they are business expenses related to that consulting activity). If those $1,350 are employee unreimbursed expenses (i.e., you were an employee for any of those clients), they are not deductible on the federal return under current law.
- Interest income from savings and investments ($1,975 total): report on Form 1040 from 1099‑INTs.
- Student loan interest ($2,700): this is an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line) claimed on Schedule 1 (Form 1040). However, the student loan interest deduction phases out by MAGI:
  - For Head of Household (same as single for this purpose) in 2023, the phase‑out is for MAGI $75,000–$90,000. Above $90,000 you cannot take the deduction.
  - With the W‑2 and self‑employment amounts you reported, your MAGI is likely above $90,000 so you may not be eligible. But half of your self‑employment tax and business deductions reduce AGI, so we should run the exact numbers to be sure.
- Charitable gift ($2,000): deductible only if you itemize; claim on Schedule A (Form 1040). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this.
- Home‑office deduction: available only if you use the space exclusively and regularly for your business and you are self‑employed (or a statutory employee). See section below for handling and calculations.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment tax on net SE income (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

2) Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- 2023 Head of Household standard deduction = $20,800.
- Your only listed itemizable amount is $2,000 charitable giving (plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, medical deductions, etc., which you did not list). State and local taxes (SALT) are capped at $10,000 total.
- Unless you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemized items not listed, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable expenses (large property taxes, mortgage interest, unreimbursed medical > 7.5% AGI, or other charitable contributions) that push your itemized total above $20,800.

3) Home‑office deduction (how to treat it)
- Qualifies only if:
  - The space is used exclusively and regularly for business, and
  - It is your principal place of business (or used for meeting patients/clients, storage of inventory in some cases).
  - It is available to self‑employed people. Employees generally cannot take it for tax years 2018–2025 (unless you are a statutory employee).
- Two methods to claim:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction.
  - Actual method: allocate actual home expenses to the business use by the percentage of your home used for business. Your percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
    - Example using only rent: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business portion of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,116.
    - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, renter’s portion of other allowable expenses. Depreciation rules apply if you own the home.
    - Important limit: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed the net profit from the business. If the deduction is larger than the business profit it may be limited and the unused portion carried forward to the next year (Form 8829 handles this).
- Which method is better? Often the actual method gives a larger deduction where rent and utilities are significant (as appears likely here), but actual requires Form 8829 and more recordkeeping. Simplified is easier and may be fine if you want simplicity.
- If your consulting work is not self‑employment (i.e., you are an employee and these are unreimbursed expenses), you cannot take a home office deduction.

4) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report adjustments (half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowable, etc.).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $15,000 consulting income and related business expenses (the $1,350 if they are business expenses).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare).
- Form 8829 — to calculate and report the home office deduction if you use the actual method.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim the $2,000 charitable donation and any SALT, etc.). If you take the standard deduction you do not use Schedule A.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to compute the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction if you’re eligible (20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C subject to limits).
- Keep 1099‑NEC (or 1099‑MISC) records for consulting income and 1099‑INTs for interest — you’ll use these to populate Form 1040/Schedules.

5) Other tax consequences to note
- Self‑employment tax: roughly 15.3% on net self‑employment income (subject to adjustments). Half of that is deductible from income (Schedule 1). You’ll likely need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for both income tax and self‑employment tax unless sufficient withholding covers it.
- QBI deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your consulting. That deduction can be valuable; it’s computed after netting business income and certain deductions and subject to taxable income thresholds.
- Student loan interest: likely phased out if your MAGI is over $90,000 (HOH), which your aggregate income suggests, but exact determination requires computing AGI after SE and other adjustments.
- State tax: don’t forget Illinois state filing and any local requirements.

