Thanks — I can help. I’ll summarize what you can likely deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, what forms you’ll need, and a few next steps/questions so I can refine numbers.

Quick facts you gave (I’ll use these for estimates):
- Filing status: Head of Household (Chicago, IL)  
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350  
- Self‑employed/consulting income: $15,000 (4 clients)  
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover savings) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975  
- Home: 690 sq ft; home office = 140 sq ft exclusive use  
- Rent: $2,100/month = $25,200/year  
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (FedLoan, 2016–2020)  
- Charitable gifts: $2,000 to Local United Way (you have receipt)  
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350

1) Which amounts are taxable income
- W‑2 wages and the consulting income and the interest/dividends are all included in gross income. Estimated total gross income ≈ $99,325.

2) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) but it phases out based on MAGI. For 2023 the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household begins at $75,000 and ends at $90,000. With your MAGI (~$99k) you are over the phaseout limit, so you likely cannot claim the student loan interest deduction.

3) Charitable donation ($2,000)
- Cash donation to a qualified charity is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. You must itemize to claim it and have the receipt — which you do.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).  
- If these are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your self‑employment consulting work, they ARE deductible on Schedule C (not Schedule A). Clarify whether the $1,350 is business expense for the consulting work.

5) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive) — how to treat
- If you use the space exclusively and regularly as your principal place of business for the self‑employment consulting, you can take a home office deduction for the self‑employment portion only (not for W‑2 employment). Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: allocate business use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%. Applied to rent ($25,200) gives ≈ $5,112 for rent portion; you could also allocate utilities and other qualifying expenses. If you use the actual method, you will typically file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C.
- The home office deduction reduces your net self‑employment income on Schedule C (so it also reduces self‑employment tax/basis for QBI).

6) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Net self‑employment income (consulting minus business expenses and home office) will be reported on Schedule C. You will also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on net earnings (roughly 15.3% on net; half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1).  
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — typically 20% of QBI for a sole proprietor if you are under the income threshold. Your overall income (~$99k) is well under the 2023 QBI thresholds, so you likely can claim up to about 20% of your qualified business income (after allowable deductions) as an additional deduction — but QBI calculations have specifics, so we can estimate once we finalize self‑employment net.

7) Itemize vs standard
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.  
- Your clear Schedule A items from the info given: charitable $2,000 (plus any state/local taxes you paid up to the SALT cap of $10k, mortgage interest, or other qualifying itemized amounts — you didn’t list any).  
- Because $2,000 is well below $20,800, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have additional significant itemized amounts (mortgage interest, SALT up to $10k, large medical expenses above threshold, etc.). Note: home office is not a Schedule A item — it’s a business expense on Schedule C.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (adjustments: half SE tax; student loan interest would go here if eligible — likely not)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $15,000 consulting and business expenses  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the half‑SE deduction  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the actual home office method (else simplified method is reported directly on Schedule C)  
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to claim the QBI deduction if you choose to take it  
- Illinois: IL‑1040 for state return (Illinois has a flat income tax — check IL instructions for reporting self‑employment income; state rules differ)

9) Practical estimate example (rough)
- If you use the actual home office allocation for rent (~$5,112) + $1,350 business expenses, net self‑employment income ≈ $15,000 − $6,462 = $8,538.  
- Self‑employment tax on that (approx) would be modest (~$1,200), half of which is deductible. QBI (20%) on the ~8,538 could be ~1,700. These flow through to reduce taxable income. (This is an illustrative calculation; send me confirmation of whether the $1,350 is business‑related and whether you want actual‑method home office, and I’ll run precise numbers.)

10) Records to keep
- W‑2, 1099s for consulting (if any), bank and investment statements (interest/dividends), receipts for charitable donations, rent receipts and proof of rent paid, documentation showing exclusive use and size of home office, receipts for any utilities you’ll allocate, records of the $1,350 expense(s), and student loan payment records (you may need these if you challenge the phaseout).

11) Next steps / questions for you
- Are the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses related to your consulting/self‑employment work or to your W‑2 job? (If consulting, they’re deductible; if W‑2, they generally are not.)  
- Do you want to use the simplified ($700) or actual method (≈$5,112 from rent alone) for the home office? If actual, do you have records for utilities, insurance, or other household costs to allocate?  
- Do you want me to estimate your federal tax liability (including self‑employment tax and potential QBI) using the actual home‑office method and the numbers above?  
- Would you like this summary sent to your email or drafted as a formal message you can share with your tax preparer? If yes, which email address should I send it to?

If you want, I can (A) run an estimated tax calculation for 2023 using the actual vs simplified home‑office method and show expected federal tax and self‑employment tax, or (B) draft a tidy email/summary you can forward to your tax preparer. Which would you prefer?

