Thanks — useful detail. I’ll summarize what you can likely deduct, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, which forms you’ll need, and a short worked example so you can see the tax impact. I’ll flag areas where I need one or two clarifying facts.

Short answers up front
- Likely best to take the 2023 head-of-household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing, based on the items you listed.
- You can and should report the $15,000 consulting as self-employment income on Schedule C and deduct ordinary business expenses there (including a home‑office share of rent or the simplified home‑office amount). That reduces both income tax and self-employment (SE) tax.
- Expect to pay self-employment tax on the net consulting profit (Schedule SE); half of that SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- The student‑loan interest deduction may be fully or partially disallowed because your income is near/above the 2023 phaseout — I’ll explain below.
- Forms you’ll likely use: 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, possibly Schedule B (for interest), Form 8995 (if claiming the QBI deduction), and state return (IL‑1040).

What you can deduct (and how)
- Business (Schedule C) — deductible from self‑employment income:
  - Ordinary business expenses you paid: your $1,350 unreimbursed expenses (if they were business expenses; “unreimbursed employee expenses” are NOT deductible — clarify if these are from the consulting work).
  - Home‑office deduction (if the space qualifies): either actual expense method (business % of rent/utilities/etc.) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft). Use whichever gives the larger net tax benefit and you can support with records.
  - Other legitimate business costs (phone, supplies, software, mileage) if applicable.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on net earnings; half of the SE tax is deductible “above the line” (on Schedule 1), lowering AGI.
- Charitable contributions: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You have $2,000 cash — that helps only if itemizing produces a larger deduction than the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible above the line, but subject to MAGI phaseouts (see below).
- Interest from banks: report on Form 1040; if interest (total) > $1,500 you must file Schedule B.

Student loan interest phaseout (important)
- For 2023, the student loan interest deduction phases out for single/head-of-household taxpayers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Your combined gross income (W‑2 + consulting + interest) is about $99,325 before adjustments — so you would likely be over the $90,000 upper limit and ineligible for the student‑loan interest deduction unless you have other above‑the‑line adjustments (pre‑tax retirement contributions, large deductible IRA, etc.) that bring MAGI below the phaseout. Also you must have actually paid the interest in 2023 and have the 1098‑E from your loan servicer. Clarify: is the $2,700 amount interest you paid during 2023, and did you receive Form 1098‑E for 2023?

Home office: simplified vs actual method (key points)
- You said home office is 140 sq ft exclusively and home is 690 sq ft → business % = 140/690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (easy, no rent records needed).
- Actual method: you can deduct ~20.29% of your rent ($2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200) → about $5,115 of rent allocable to the home office (plus a share of utilities if you have them).
- For a self‑employed taxpayer the home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit (and therefore reduces SE tax and income tax). Use whichever method you can support and that yields the bigger tax benefit — often the actual method yields a bigger deduction for renters with a large office share, but you must have documentation (rent receipts, utilities, floor plan showing exclusive use, etc.). I can help compute both and compare if you want.

Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- If your consulting is a qualified trade or business, you may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995). With taxable income likely below the higher limits for service businesses, you’ll probably be able to take a simple QBI calculation. QBI is calculated after adjustments (so it depends on your Schedule C net profit after the home-office and other business expenses).

Itemize vs Standard deduction
- Standard deduction (HOH, 2023) = $20,800.
- From what you listed, itemized items we can count with certainty are: charitable $2,000 and state income tax/property tax up to SALT cap ($10,000). Illinois state income tax on your income will be much less than $10,000 (rough estimate ~ $4,900), so itemizing would likely total roughly $6–7k unless you have mortgage interest, large property taxes, unreimbursed business expenses for employees (not deductible currently), or big medical expenses. That is well below the standard deduction — so standard deduction is likely the better choice. Note: even if you take the standard deduction, your Schedule C deductions (home office, business expenses) still reduce your AGI.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income / adjustments — e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowed)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $15,000 gross, subtract business expenses and home-office allocation
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — if total interest > $1,500 (your interest total ~$1,975)
  - Form 8995 (QBI deduction) if you take QBI (simple version usually OK if under thresholds)
  - Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not)
- State:
  - Illinois Form IL‑1040 — include your combined income; IL taxes taxable income at the flat rate.

Rough worked example (two home‑office options) — quick approximations
- Totals you gave: W‑2 $82,350 + consulting $15,000 + interest $1,975 = gross income ≈ $99,325.
- Option 1 — Actual home‑office (≈20.29% of rent):
  - Home‑office rent deduction ≈ $5,114; other business expenses $1,350 → Schedule C net ≈ $8,536.
  - SE tax (approx) ≈ $1,206 (you pay this; half ≈ $603 is an above‑the‑line deduction).
  - AGI after half SE deduction ≈ $98,722 (before any student loan interest adjustment).
  - Standard deduction $20,800 → taxable income ≈ $77,900 (then QBI may reduce taxable income further).
  - Estimated total federal tax + SE tax ~ roughly $12k (very approximate).
- Option 2 — Simplified home‑office ($5/sq ft = $700):
  - Net Schedule C ≈ $12,950 → SE tax ≈ $1,830 (half ≈ $915 deduction).
  - AGI after half SE deduction ≈ $98,410 → taxable income after standard ≈ $77,600 (less QBI).
  - Estimated total federal tax + SE tax ~ roughly $12.4k (approx).
- Conclusion from the example: the actual method is likely to save you a few hundred dollars versus simplified given the large rent share — but actual method requires documentation.

Questions / items I need from you to firm this up
1. Is the $2,700 student loan interest amount paid in tax year 2023? Did you receive Form 1098‑E for 2023?
2. Was the $1,350 in “unreimbursed expenses” incurred for the consulting (self‑employment) work? Or were they employee unreimbursed expenses? (Only self‑employment business expenses are deductible on Schedule C.)
3. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC for the $15,000 consulting? Any business‑related receipts for rent, utilities, or other home/office costs?
4. Did you make any estimated federal or state tax payments in 2023? Do you have federal withholding on the W‑2 (amount)?
5. Do you want me to compute a more exact estimate of federal tax (including QBI) using either the actual home‑office method or the simplified method?

Suggested next steps
- Gather W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT(s), 1098‑E, receipts for charitable, business expenses, rent/utility bills, and any estimated tax payment records.
- If you want, I can: (a) compute a more precise tax estimate for 2023 using the actual vs simplified home‑office methods, (b) prepare a checklist of documents for your tax preparer, or (c) draft suggested Schedule C / summary lines you can give to your preparer.

