Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s generally allowed, whether you’ll likely itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll call out assumptions; if you want exact numbers I can recompute with any extra details (state tax withheld, whether the $1,350 are business expenses, mortgage interest, utility amounts, etc.).

Quick summary (top-line)
- Your W-2 and consulting are taxed differently: W-2 wages go on Form 1040; the $15,000 consulting is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C and Schedule SE (self‑employment tax).
- Home office deduction is available for self‑employed work if the space is used exclusively and regularly. You can use the simplified method or actual‑expense method. Given your rent, actual expenses will likely give a larger deduction.
- Student loan interest: the deduction is an “above the line” adjustment (up to $2,500) but phases out with MAGI. With your reported income, you’re likely above the phaseout for head of household — so you probably cannot take it, but we should calculate MAGI to be sure.
- Itemize vs standard: 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800. With only $2,000 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest listed, you’re very likely better off taking the standard deduction.
- Key tax forms you’ll use: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, (Form 8829 if you use actual home expenses), Schedule 1 (for adjustments like half SE tax and student loan interest if eligible), and IL‑1040 for Illinois. If you itemize (unlikely) you’d use Schedule A.

Detailed breakdown and calculations (with assumptions)

1) Income
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment (consulting): $15,000 → report on Schedule C
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover) + $195 (Fidelity) → reported on Form 1040 (1099‑INTs)
Estimated total gross income ≈ $99,325.

2) Self‑employment treatment
- Report consulting on Schedule C. Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (e.g., supplies, travel for clients), report them on Schedule C. If the $1,350 are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your 2023 return (suspended).
- Pay self‑employment tax on net earnings: use Schedule SE. SE tax is 15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE income; you get to deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

Example (illustrative):
- If $1,350 are business expenses, net self‑employment income before home office = 15,000 − 1,350 = 13,650.

3) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft)
- Business use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%.
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
- Actual‑expenses method: allocate 20.29% of qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, homeowner’s insurance portion, etc.). If rent = $2,100/month → annual rent = $25,200 → rent portion for home office ≈ 0.2029 × 25,200 ≈ $5,112 (plus pro rata utilities, etc.). So actual method would likely give a bigger deduction than simplified.
- To use actual expenses you’ll use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and then transfer the allowed deduction to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method you do not file Form 8829.
- Important limit: The home office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business. If home office + other business deductions exceed business gross income, the excess home office expense is carried forward to future years.

4) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (above-the-line) if you meet MAGI limits. For 2023 the phaseout for single/head of household starts at MAGI $75,000 and phases out to $90,000 (if your MAGI is at/above $90,000 you cannot claim it). With your rough gross income (~$99k) you’re probably above the $90k phaseout and thus ineligible — but exact eligibility depends on your MAGI (AGI before this deduction but after certain adjustments). I recommend calculating MAGI precisely to confirm.

5) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction (2023, Head of Household) = $20,800.
- Known potential itemized deductions you listed: charitable $2,000. Possibly state income tax/property tax paid (SALT) up to $10,000 cap — but unless you have significant deductible state/local taxes and mortgage interest, your itemized total will likely be well under $20,800. So you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.

6) Other practical points
- 1099s / reporting: Make sure you have 1099‑INTs for interest and any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for consulting if clients issued them. Even if no 1099 was issued you must report the $15,000.
- Estimated tax: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties if your withholding doesn’t cover your tax liability.
- State return: File Illinois Form IL‑1040. Illinois taxes income; check state-specific deductions/credits.
- Records to keep: receipts for the charitable donation, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E), records for home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance), receipts for business expenses, and any 1099s.

Which forms you’ll likely need
- 2023 Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like half of SE tax, student loan interest if eligible
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual expenses method for home office
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you won’t)
- Illinois IL‑1040 (state return)
- Form 1040‑ES (if you need to make estimated tax payments)

Recommended next steps (I can help)
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the consulting work (if yes, they go on Schedule C; if not, they’re not deductible).
2. Gather exact amounts for: state income tax paid (withheld/estimated), any mortgage interest (if applicable), utilities and other home expenses (if you want to use actual home office method), and the student loan interest 1098‑E.
3. If you’d like, I can:
   - Run precise calculations (net SE income, SE tax, effect of home office simplified vs actual, estimated AGI and whether student loan interest qualifies).
   - Draft a summary you can give to your tax preparer, or draft an email to your accountant with these numbers.
   - Create a checklist of documents to collect.

Would you like me to calculate precise amounts (SE tax, income tax impact, and confirm whether the student loan deduction phases out) if you confirm whether the $1,350 are business expenses and provide state tax withheld for 2023 (or estimated state tax paid)?

