Thanks — good detail. I can walk through what’s likely deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll typically need. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end so I can give tighter numbers.

Short answer / likely outcome
- You’ll generally file Form 1040 as Head of Household.  
- Most likely you’ll take the 2023 standard deduction for HOH ($20,800) unless you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other big itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.  
- Your $15,000 consulting income should be reported on Schedule C (self‑employment) if it was earned as an independent contractor. That lets you deduct business expenses (including a home office portion and qualifying unreimbursed expenses that are business-related).  
- Interest from Discover + Fidelity (total $1,975) is taxable interest and will likely require Schedule B (threshold > $1,500).  
- The student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 but phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023. Based on the incomes you listed, you are likely above the phaseout and therefore probably cannot claim this deduction.  
- You’ll also need Schedule SE to calculate self‑employment tax on net consulting income. Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040/Schedule 1.

Detailed breakdown

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Metro Consulting: $82,350 — reported on Form 1040 (Box amounts on the W‑2).  
- Consulting income $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Form 1040). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them; even if you didn’t get 1099s, you must report the income.  
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975 — report as taxable interest on Form 1040; Schedule B is required if total interest > $1,500.

2) Home office
- Requirement: exclusive and regular use of that space for business and used as your principal place of business (you indicated 140 sq ft exclusive — that generally qualifies).  
- Methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 deduction on Schedule C.
  - Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to business. Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%. You can apply that percentage to rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Based on rent alone: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 × 20.3% ≈ $5,112. If you have utilities, internet, and renter’s insurance, a larger share could be deductible. Use Form 8829 (if you itemize the actual method for self‑employment).
- Note: Only used for self‑employed Schedule C filers; employees generally cannot deduct unreimbursed home office expenses under current federal law (2018–2025 suspension).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are employee unreimbursed business expenses (related to your W‑2 job), they are not deductible on your federal return for 2018–2025.  
- If they’re business expenses related to your consulting work, report them on Schedule C (they reduce your self‑employment net income).

4) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid to FedLoan 2016–2020)
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line). BUT the deduction phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000–$90,000 (2023). Your preliminary MAGI (W‑2 + consulting + interest) is about $99,325, which is above the upper threshold — so you likely cannot claim any student loan interest deduction for 2023. If you want, I can walk through a more exact MAGI calculation if you have additional adjustments.

5) Charitable donation ($2,000)
- Deductible only if you itemize. With the HOH standard deduction at $20,800, you would need total itemized deductions to exceed $20,800 to make itemizing worthwhile. Charitable of $2,000 by itself won’t get you there unless you also have sizable mortgage interest, high state/local tax payments (SALT up to $10,000 limit), or other itemizable amounts.

6) State & local taxes (SALT)
- Not provided. State income tax and property taxes count toward SALT but are capped at $10,000 total (federal limitation). Include those when deciding whether to itemize.

7) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net SE earnings after the 92.35% adjustment). You pay SE tax on Schedule SE; half of the SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1 (Form 1040) as an adjustment to income.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- You may be eligible for a Section 199A QBI deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) depending on final taxable income and the nature of the consulting business. That can reduce taxable income further. It has complex thresholds; I can estimate if you want.

Which tax forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (if adjustments such as half of SE tax are needed)  
- Schedule B (interest income, because total interest > $1,500)  
