- Which to pick: actual method looks much larger here (≈$5,114 vs $700). Use Form 8829 (or report on Schedule C) to compute actual method. Note: the home office deduction cannot be used for an employee’s unreimbursed expenses — it must be for self‑employment.

Self‑employment tax and effect on AGI
- You’ll report business revenue and allowed business expenses on Schedule C to get net profit.
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is computed on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Home-office deduction and other business expenses reduce your Schedule C profit (which reduces both income tax and SE tax).
- Important limit: the home‑office deduction and other business expenses cannot reduce allowed business income below allowable rules in some cases — but in normal small‑business situations they reduce net profit and therefore reduce both income and SE tax.

Student loan interest deduction
- Maximum allowed deduction = $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1). You reported $2,700 paid, so the maximum before MAGI phaseout is $2,500.
- Phaseout for 2023 (single/head of household): MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000; deduction is eliminated at or above the top of the range.
- Your likely MAGI estimate (roughly): W‑2 $82,350 + consulting net income (after business expenses) minus half SE tax → your MAGI is likely at or above the $90k phaseout threshold unless your business expenses are large. So the student loan interest deduction will likely be reduced or eliminated. I can run a tighter estimate if you confirm the expenses and whether you’ll use the actual or simplified home office method.

Unreimbursed expenses — clarify
- If the $1,350 is unreimbursed employee business expenses (for a W‑2 job), those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If the $1,350 are business expenses for your consulting (self‑employment), they are deductible on Schedule C. Please confirm which they are.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your only listed itemizable amount is $2,000 charitable giving (plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc. you haven’t mentioned). Unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (SALT up to $10k, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses above 7.5% AGI, etc.), the standard deduction will almost certainly be better.
- Therefore you will likely take the standard deduction and report the charity only if you can aggregate more itemizable amounts that exceed $20,800.

