Thanks — good detail. Below is a clear, practical breakdown of what you can likely deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal forms you’ll use. I’ll flag a few clarifying questions at the end so I can firm up numbers.

Short summary / recommendation
- Most likely you should take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800). Your likely itemizable deductions (you only listed $2,000 of charitable cash) are far below the standard deduction.
- The $15,000 of consulting income is treated as self‑employment income. If so, you can claim a home‑office deduction (and other business expenses related to that consulting) on Schedule C, which will reduce your self‑employment tax and AGI.
- The student‑loan interest ($2,700) is an above‑the‑line deduction only if your MAGI is within the allowable range. With your wages and additional income, your MAGI will likely be above the phaseout range for the student-loan interest deduction, so you likely cannot take it — but see clarifying Qs below to confirm.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for traditional W‑2 employment) are not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 is a self‑employment/business expense for your consulting, it is deductible on Schedule C; if it’s an unreimbursed employee expense from your W‑2 job, it’s not deductible.

More detail and rough numbers (estimates)
Assumptions I used: W‑2 wages = $82,350; consulting gross = $15,000; interest income = $1,780 + $195 = $1,975. I’ll show two scenarios depending on whether the $1,350 is business-related.

- Home size and allocation:
  - Home office = 140 sq ft; total home = 690 sq ft → business percentage ≈ 140 / 690 ≈ 20.3%.

- Home‑office deduction (actual method on rent): 
  - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
  - Rent portion allocable to home office ≈ 25,200 × 20.3% ≈ $5,112.
  - Alternatively, the IRS simplified method = $5 × 140 sq ft = $700. (Actual method here likely gives a larger deduction because you rent.)

- Scenario A — $1,350 is a consulting (business) expense:
  - Business expenses = home office $5,112 + other business expenses $1,350 = $6,462.
  - Net SE income ≈ 15,000 − 6,462 = $8,538.
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 8,538) ≈ $2,119 × scaled down ≈ ~$1,206. (Exact = about $1,206.)
  - Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $603 (an adjustment to income).
  - Rough AGI ≈ 82,350 + 1,975 + 8,538 − 603 ≈ $92,260.
  - Student loan interest phaseout: For single/HOH 2023 the phaseout is roughly $75k–$90k MAGI; your MAGI here (~$92k) is above the phaseout, so you likely cannot claim the student‑loan interest deduction.
  - Standard deduction (HOH) $20,800 → taxable income ≈ $92,260 − $20,800 ≈ $71,460.

- Scenario B — $1,350 is unreimbursed employee expense (not deductible):
  - Business expenses = home office $5,112 only.
  - Net SE income ≈ 15,000 − 5,112 = $9,888.
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 9,888) ≈ ~$1,398; half deductible ≈ $699.
  - Rough AGI ≈ 82,350 + 1,975 + 9,888 − 699 ≈ $93,514.
  - Student‑loan interest still phased out (MAGI > $90k).
  - Taxable income after HOH standard deduction ≈ $93,514 − $20,800 ≈ $72,714.

Key takeaways from the numbers
- The home office deduction (if you use the actual method and allocate rent/utilities) will give meaningful tax benefit because of your high rent; use the actual method if you have supporting bills. If you use the simplified method, you get only $700.
- The student‑loan interest deduction is probably not allowed because your MAGI exceeds the phaseout range; a precise MAGI calculation might change this if there are other adjustments I don’t know about.
- Itemizing is very unlikely to beat the $20,800 HOH standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable items (mortgage interest, significant state+local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, or much more charitable giving). From what you listed, standard deduction is better.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not allowed as itemized deductions for 2018–2025. Only business expenses for the consulting work (reported on Schedule C) reduce taxable income.

Forms and filings you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — used for some adjustments, but much of the self‑employment reporting is on Schedule C.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report the consulting income and business expenses (including home office allocation if self‑employed).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for business use of home (home office). If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829; instead report the simplified deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely here).
- State return: Illinois IL‑1040 (you’ll also report wages and other income per IL rules).

Other practical points & recordkeeping
- Keep documentation for the home office (floor plan/measurements showing exclusive/regular use), rent receipts, utility bills, renter’s insurance portion, and any business receipts.
- Keep the charitable receipt for the $2,000 gift (must have written acknowledgement for single contributions ≥ $250).
- If you owe more than about $1,000 in tax after withholding, you may owe estimated tax penalties — consider making quarterly estimated payments for your self‑employment income (use Form 1040‑ES).
- Self‑employment tax is separate from income tax; the Schedule SE calculates it and half is deductible for income tax purposes.
- If you’re close to phaseout thresholds or have other adjustments (HSA, IRA contributions, retirement deferrals, student loan timing differences), those can change whether you get the student‑loan interest deduction or other benefits.

Clarifying questions (so I can give exact guidance or compute final numbers)
1. Is the $15,000 consulting amount gross receipts or net after business expenses?  
2. Is the $1,350 unreimbursed expense related to that consulting (i.e., business expense) or from your W‑2 job?  
