While you answer, here are clear, practical rules and estimates based on the details you gave.

1) Which items are (generally) deductible
- Consulting income ($15,000): Treated as self‑employment income if you received 1099s or worked as an independent contractor. You report net profit on Schedule C; allowable business expenses reduce that profit.
- Interest from Discover and Fidelity ($1,780 + $195): Taxable interest/dividend income — report on Form 1040 (1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).
- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid): Up to $2,500 may be deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment to income (Form 1040, Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts (see next).
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Local United Way): Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this (no general above‑the‑line deduction in 2023).
- $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses”:
  - If they are business expenses for your consulting (supplies, client travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal tax purposes for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive use): Deductible only if you are self‑employed (used for Schedule C). Not deductible for an employee. See details below.

2) Student loan interest phaseout (important)
- For 2023, the student loan interest deduction phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. If your MAGI (see note) is at or above $90,000, the deduction is fully phased out.
- MAGI for this purpose is your income after most adjustments but before the student‑loan interest deduction itself; self‑employment adjustments etc. can affect it. Because you have consulting/self‑employment income, whether you have net profit after business and home‑office deductions will determine whether you’re inside the phaseout.
- If your MAGI ends up above $90,000, you would not get the student loan interest deduction.

3) Home office: how to treat and rough numbers
- Requirements: The space must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). You stated “140 sq ft exclusive,” which implies you meet the exclusive‑use test.
- Employee vs. self‑employed: Only available to self‑employed taxpayers (Schedule C). If your consulting is self‑employment, you can claim it; if you’re an employee, you cannot.
- Methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft = 140 * $5 = $700.
  - Regular (actual expenses): Business percentage of actual home costs. Business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. If rent is $2,100/month = $25,200/year, the home‑office portion of rent = 25,200 * 0.2029 ≈ $5,116. You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase this.
- Important limits:
  - The home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business. If your net profit (before the home office deduction) is small, the deductible amount may be limited; excess can often carry forward (for Form 8829 regular method).
  - If you use the regular method you typically file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). With the simplified method, you enter the deduction directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Based on your numbers, the regular method (rent portion ≈ $5,116) will likely be larger than the simplified method ($700) — but only if your consulting business net income is high enough to absorb it.

4) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction (Head of Household) for 2023 = $20,800.
- Your likely itemizable deductions from the information provided are low: the charitable gift $2,000 (and possibly any state/local taxes paid up to the SALT $10k cap, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc. — you didn’t list those).
- Unless you have significant additional deductible items (large state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, other charitable gifts), the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than itemizing with only $2,000 in charity.
- So my preliminary conclusion: you will very likely take the standard deduction. Exception: if you have significant state taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t list, re‑check itemizing.

5) Self‑employment tax and estimated payments
- If the consulting is self‑employment, you will owe self‑employment tax (~15.3%) on net self‑employment earnings; you calculate this on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of the SE tax as an above‑the‑line deduction.
- You may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments in 2024 if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file.

6) Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 — main return.
  - Schedule 1 (if adjustments such as student loan interest or one‑half SE tax are needed).
  - Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (if consulting is self‑employment).
  - Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (if Schedule C shows profit).
  - Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method for home‑office deductions). If you use the simplified method, you do not need Form 8829.
  - Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize).
  - 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV — attach/report amounts as appropriate (these are information slips you’ll receive and use to fill the 1040).
- Illinois:
  - IL‑1040 — Illinois individual income tax return. Illinois taxes income, so include your federal taxable income information as required. Illinois has a flat rate and different rules for deductions and exemptions — you’ll report your federal AGI or taxable income as part of the IL return per IL instructions.

7) Recommended next steps (practical)
- Confirm whether the $1,350 are business expenses for the consulting work or unreimbursed employee expenses.
- Gather documents: W‑2(s), any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for consulting, 1099‑INTs/1099‑DIVs for bank/fidelity income, proof of charitable donation (receipt), student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E) or lender statement, rent receipts/records and utility bills for the home office allocation.
- Decide home‑office method: simplified is easier but likely smaller; regular gives bigger deduction if your net business profit supports it.
