Summary / key points
- Filing status: Head of household (standard deduction for 2023 = $20,800).
- Income to report: W‑2 wages $82,350; self‑employment consulting gross $15,000; interest $1,975 (Discover + Fidelity). Total gross ≈ $99,325 before business deductions.
- Self‑employment: Report your consulting on Schedule C. Business expenses (the $1,350 if they’re business expenses) reduce Schedule C profit.
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe SE tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). Half of the SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction that reduces AGI.
- Home office: Because you’re self‑employed and you say the office is used exclusively (140 sq ft), you can take a home‑office deduction. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Regular: allocate actual home costs (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) by percentage of home used for business. Your office is 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29% of the home. With $2,100/mo rent (annual $25,200) the rent share alone would be about $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,120 (plus a share of utilities if applicable). Regular method usually gives a larger deduction here.
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (for 2023). With your income mix, your MAGI is likely above $90k, so you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction — but I can check precisely after we pick the home‑office method and confirm the $ amounts paid in 2023.
- Charitable gift ($2,000): Deductible only if you itemize. Because the HOH standard deduction ($20,800) is much larger than your listed itemizable amounts (only $2,000 charitable listed and no large mortgage interest shown), you’ll probably take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

Rough will‑it‑help calculations (estimates to show impact)
Assumptions: $1,350 is business expense; interest = $1,975.

1) If you use the simplified home‑office method ($700):
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 15,000 − 1,350 − 700 = $12,950.
- Estimated self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × $12,950 ≈ $1,830 (rounding).
- Half of that (~$915) is an adjustment to income.
- Estimated AGI ≈ (W‑2 82,350 + interest 1,975 + net SE 12,950) − half SE tax 915 ≈ $96,360.
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $96,360 − $20,800 = $75,560.

2) If you use the regular home‑office method (rent share ≈ $5,120):
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 15,000 − 1,350 − 5,120 = $8,530.
- Estimated self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × $8,530 ≈ $1,206.
- Half (~$603) is an adjustment.
- Estimated AGI ≈ (82,350 + 1,975 + 8,530) − 603 ≈ $92,252.
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $92,252 − $20,800 = $71,452.

Takeaway from the estimates:
- Regular method (using actual rent share) very likely gives a larger deduction than the simplified method and reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax. Given your high monthly rent, regular method will probably be better.
- In either scenario, your AGI is likely above $90k, so you will likely be phased out of the student‑loan interest deduction for 2023.
- With only $2,000 in charitable gifts and no other big itemizable amounts shown, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.

Important tax rules to note
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if those $1,350 are for a W‑2 job) are not deductible (suspended through 2025).
- Home office deduction is only available for the self‑employed (Schedule C). It requires regular and exclusive business use; keep photos and a floor plan and copies of rent/utilities to support the deduction.
- If you use the regular home‑office method you must file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your return.
