I can walk through this and give practical next steps — but I’m not a CPA or tax attorney. This is general guidance; please confirm with a tax pro (or let me email one for you—I can reach out to vince2000@gmail.com if you’d like).

Quick summary (based on what you gave)
- Income to report: W-2 wages $82,350; self‑employment consulting $15,000; interest $1,780 (Discover 1099‑INT); dividends $195 (Fidelity). All of these must be reported on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment: The $15,000 is reported on Schedule C (if you are a sole proprietor). You’ll deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including the home‑office allocation if you use it for the consulting business). You’ll also file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (and can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040).
- Home office: You have two options — simplified or actual method. Simplified: 140 sq ft × $5 = $700. Actual method: business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. If you rent $2,100/month (annual rent $25,200), the rent portion allocable to the office ≈ $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115; you could also allocate part of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc., to increase the deduction. The home‑office deduction is available only for self‑employed (Schedule C) — not for W‑2 employees (except very narrow exceptions).
- Deductions and whether to itemize: For 2023 the standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800. Your known potential itemized deductions now look small: charitable $2,000 (only deductible if you itemize), no mortgage interest listed, no property tax listed, etc. The home office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it’s a business deduction reducing your self‑employment net income. Given the numbers you’ve provided, it’s very likely the standard deduction ($20,800) will be larger than your itemized deductions. So most likely you’ll take the standard deduction and still deduct business expenses (including home office) on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: Only interest paid in the tax year (2023) can be deducted as an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). You mentioned $2,700 from FedLoan 2016–2020 — if that interest was paid in prior years, you cannot deduct it on your 2023 return. For 2023, the student loan interest deduction phases out for single/head‑of‑household taxpayers with MAGI roughly between $75,000 and $90,000 (partial to full phaseout). Given your combined income (W‑2 + interest/dividends + net self‑employment), you may be above the phaseout range and might not be eligible; we can estimate more precisely if you confirm the actual 2023 payments and business expenses.
- Unreimbursed expenses $1,350: If these were employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). If those $1,350 are expenses of your self‑employment consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Forms you will likely need:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $15,000 consulting and related expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for home office; not required for the simplified method
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required if total interest/dividends exceed $1,500 (your interest alone is $1,780, so yes)
  - Form 1040-ES (for estimated quarterly tax payments) — if you need to pay estimated taxes for self‑employment income
  - Any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC you received from clients (report even if you didn’t get a 1099)
  - Keep receipts for charitable contributions (you said you have the receipt for the $2,000), business expenses, rent/utility bills, and student loan interest statements (1098‑E if available)
- Estimated tax and withholding: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes and should make sure enough tax is withheld (or pay estimated taxes) to avoid penalties.

Key calculations and examples
- Business percentage (home office) = 140 / 690 = 0.2029 (≈20.29%).
- Simplified home‑office deduction = 140 × $5 = $700.
- Example actual rent portion (annual): $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,115 (plus utilities portion if applicable).
