Below is a clear summary of what likely applies, what forms you’ll need, and recommended next steps.

1) Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- For 2023 the standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800.
- Your likely itemizable items (cash charity $2,000, possible state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest if any) look small compared with the $20,800 standard deduction. So in most realistic scenarios with only the $2,000 charitable gift and no large mortgage interest or big SALT payments, you will take the standard deduction.
- Note: the home office deduction and self‑employment business expenses are not itemized deductions — they reduce self‑employment income on Schedule C and thus reduce AGI/ taxable income even if you take the standard deduction.

2) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 per year, subject to MAGI phaseouts.
- For 2023 the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household starts at MAGI $75,000 and fully phases out at $90,000. Given your W‑2 ($82,350) plus $15,000 consulting and ~ $1,975 interest, your MAGI before business expense and SE adjustments will be above $90k — you may be phased out and not be able to claim the deduction.
- However, legitimate business expenses (including properly calculated home office expenses) and the half of self‑employment tax that’s deductible will reduce MAGI. Whether you qualify depends on exact numbers. Please confirm the year you paid the $2,700 and we can run a quick estimate.

3) Self‑employment income, business expenses, and the home office
- You report the $15,000 consulting on Schedule C. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses (including business portion of rent/utilities if you choose actual home office method, supplies, phone, the $1,350 if it’s a business expense, etc.) reduce Schedule C net profit.
- Self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) is computed on net Schedule C profit. You pay SE tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings), but half of SE tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Home office: you appear to meet the requirements (exclusive and regular business use). Two choices:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft that’s 140 * $5 = $700 deduction.
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business. Your business percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. Example: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business portion of rent = 25,200 × 20.29% ≈ $5,111 (plus proportionate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if eligible). To use actual expenses you generally file Form 8829 (for business use of home) with your return; simplified method requires no Form 8829.
- Practical point: If you use the actual method and have other deductible business expenses (the $1,350 if it’s business‑related), your Schedule C net income will shrink — that both reduces income tax and SE tax. If the business loss becomes large enough it can affect other tax items (and there are limits if you have multiple businesses), so keep records.

4) Treatment of the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses from a W‑2 job: under current law (through 2025) miscellaneous employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible on your federal return (with narrow exceptions).
- If they are related to your consulting (self‑employment) they’re deductible on Schedule C. Please confirm the nature of these expenses.

5) Forms you will likely need for 2023 federal return
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., deductible part of SE tax, student loan interest)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) to report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual home‑office method (not needed for simplified method)
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given the standard deduction)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC receipts or records of consulting income (you must report all income even if you did not receive a 1099)
- 1099‑INT (from Discover, Fidelity) and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if you received them
- Illinois state return (IL‑1040) — Illinois taxes apply; be sure to include state filing

6) Quick rough numbers (illustrative)
- Rent annual = $25,200. Home office actual share ≈ $5,111. Simplified home office = $700.
- If you claim actual home office plus $1,350 other business expenses, your net self‑employment income could drop significantly (which reduces SE tax and may help you qualify for the student loan interest deduction). If you take the simplified method, the business deduction is smaller.
- Because the home office deduction is taken on Schedule C, it does not count toward Schedule A itemized deductions — so taking the home office deduction does not make itemizing more attractive for the standard deduction decision.

7) Recommendations / next steps
