Below is a clear, practical summary of how your 2023 items are treated, plus numbers and the forms you’ll likely need. This is general information — for a binding determination or complex planning, consult a CPA or tax professional.

1) Income — what to report
- W-2 wages: Metro Consulting $82,350 — report on Form 1040 (Box 1 wages).
- Consulting $15,000: treated as self-employment/business income if you were an independent contractor (1099-NEC or not). Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If those were employer wages, they’d be W-2, but you said consulting so I’ll assume self-employed.
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover) + $195 (Fidelity) = $1,975 taxable interest. Because taxable interest > $1,500, you must file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) with Form 1040.

2) Self-employment tax
- Net profit from consulting (Schedule C) is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You pay SE tax on ~92.35% of net self-employment earnings; the SE tax rate is 15.3%. You also get to deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).

3) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive use; total home 690 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo = $25,200/yr)
- Eligible only if you use the office regularly and exclusively for your self-employed consulting business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 * $5 = $700 deduction. No Form 8829 required.
  - Regular (actual) method: business-use percentage = 140 / 690 ≈ 20.29%. You allocate a share of household expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) to business. Example just for rent: 25,200 * 20.29% ≈ $5,118 of rent allocable to home office (plus your share of utilities, etc.). To use actual method you complete Form 8829 and attach it to Schedule C.
- Practical note: the home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus SE tax). The deduction cannot be used unless the space is used exclusively for business.

4) Unreimbursed expenses $1,350
- If those expenses are business expenses for your consulting work (supplies, travel for consulting, phone portion, etc.), include them on Schedule C and they reduce your net self-employment income.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, those are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), so they would not be deductible on your federal return.

5) Student loan interest $2,700 (FedLoan)
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (an adjustment to income). However it phases out at higher incomes.
- For 2023, the phaseout for single / head of household is MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (if MAGI is at or above $90k you cannot claim it). Your rough gross income: 82,350 (W-2) + 15,000 (consulting) + 1,975 (interest) = $99,325 before adjustments. After adjustments (half SE tax, etc.) your MAGI will still likely be above $90k. That means you will probably be ineligible to claim the student loan interest deduction for 2023. Recommendation: calculate MAGI precisely (or run tax software / ask a CPA) because adjustments like half SE tax and above-the-line deductions can change the result slightly.

6) Charity $2,000 to United Way
- Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and the organization is a qualified charity (keep the receipt).
- Standard deduction for 2023 for Head of Household is $20,800. You should itemize only if your total itemized deductions (charity + mortgage interest + state/local taxes up to $10k SALT cap + medical exceeding threshold, etc.) exceed $20,800. With only $2,000 of charitable gifts shown, you’re almost certainly better off taking the standard deduction unless you have substantial other deductible items (mortgage interest, significant state & local tax payments up to the $10k cap, large medical expenses, etc.).

7) Quick example calculation (illustrative)
- Gross income = $82,350 + $15,000 + $1,975 = $99,325.
- Suppose you use actual home-office and allocate $5,118 rent and have $1,350 business expenses → that reduces your consulting profit: 15,000 - 5,118 - 1,350 = $8,532 net self-employment profit.
- Self-employment taxable portion ≈ 8,532 * 0.9235 = 7,883 ⇒ SE tax ≈ 7,883 * 15.3% ≈ $1,205. Half SE tax deductible ≈ $602. (These are approximate — actual SE tax calculation uses Schedule SE forms and rounding.)
- AGI then roughly = 99,325 - 602 (half SE tax) - other above-the-line adjustments. Then subtract standard deduction $20,800. That gives a rough taxable-income starting point. (I can run a more exact estimate if you want.)

8) Forms you’ll likely use for 2023 (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like half of SE tax, possibly student loan interest (if eligible).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and half deduction.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual home-office method.
- Schedule B — because your taxable interest > $1,500.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not, see above).
- Illinois state return: IL-1040 (file Illinois state income tax). Illinois has a flat tax — make sure to include your federal AGI-based data on IL-1040.

9) Other practical items & recommendations
- Keep receipts and good records for the home-office calculation (floor plan / measurements, rent, utilities bills), business expenses, and the charitable contribution receipt.
- If you haven’t been making estimated tax payments during the year, you may owe underpayment penalties because of self-employment tax and income tax on the consulting income. Consider making estimated payments for the current year going forward.
- If any clients issued 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, make sure you have those forms; even without 1099s you must report the income.
- Consider using tax software or a CPA — given the self-employment income + home office + possible phaseout of student loan interest, a CPA can help minimize mistakes and estimate taxes/quarterly payments.

10) Questions I need to be more precise / next steps
- Were the consulting payments reported on 1099-NEC? Are they definitely self-employment (not W-2 contractor)?
- Are the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses for consulting or for your W-2 job?
- Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes, or other deductions that might make itemizing worthwhile?
- Do you want me to run a more detailed, line-by-line estimate of your 2023 federal tax liability (I can do that if you confirm the items above)?

